1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,687
Meet Silicon Cascade, a fictional manufacturer.

2
00:00:03,907 --> 00:00:09,667
A customer needs one thousand microcontrollers, but the warehouse holds only three hundred and fifty.

3
00:00:09,887 --> 00:00:13,364
Then an upstream supply interruption puts the order at risk.

4
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:17,001
Here is the shortfall.

5
00:00:17,221 --> 00:00:19,141
Each square represents fifty chips.

6
00:00:19,361 --> 00:00:21,132
Inventory fills seven squares.

7
00:00:21,352 --> 00:00:22,803
Thirteen remain empty.

8
00:00:23,023 --> 00:00:26,393
Recovery has to find another six hundred and fifty units.

9
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:32,113
In this synthetic scenario, a Taiwan foundry goes down.

10
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:34,509
The normal supply route is interrupted.

11
00:00:34,729 --> 00:00:36,863
The factory turns to backup suppliers.

12
00:00:37,083 --> 00:00:41,435
But two supplier names do not necessarily mean two independent sources.

13
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,860
This is the real Anchor web app, exploring the synthetic supply network.

14
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:49,659
The map provides context.

15
00:00:49,879 --> 00:00:53,569
The dependency paths provide the evidence for the recovery decision.

16
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,836
Anchor follows connected evidence in Falkor D B.

17
00:00:59,056 --> 00:01:01,445
Backup B depends on the Taiwan foundry.

18
00:01:01,665 --> 00:01:02,881
So does Backup C.

19
00:01:03,101 --> 00:01:05,533
Their paths converge on the same failed source.

20
00:01:05,753 --> 00:01:09,679
One upstream outage can therefore affect both apparent alternatives.

21
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:13,513
Pause on the actual returned paths.

22
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:15,888
Both backups point to the same foundry.

23
00:01:16,108 --> 00:01:19,415
These recorded relationships make the shared risk visible.

24
00:01:19,635 --> 00:01:23,347
A different supplier name does not establish an independent source.

25
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:34,041
The recorded optimizer candidate takes three hundred and fifty units from inventory, plus six hundred and fifty from independent Supplier D.

26
00:01:34,261 --> 00:01:40,171
That fills the one thousand unit order, at a synthetic cost of five thousand eight hundred and fifty dollars.

27
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:43,036
This is a candidate, not proof of execution.

28
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,791
A candidate still needs to be challenged.

29
00:01:48,011 --> 00:01:49,419
What if capacity falls?

30
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:51,132
What if another source fails?

31
00:01:51,352 --> 00:01:54,893
Anchor uses a declared, finite set of modeled scenarios.

32
00:01:55,113 --> 00:01:58,825
Passing those cases does not establish universal resilience.

33
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:02,428
Now the new command line test.

34
00:02:02,648 --> 00:02:05,741
Execution and read-back return verified true.

35
00:02:05,961 --> 00:02:08,287
But look at the receipt status: duplicate.

36
00:02:08,507 --> 00:02:10,363
The quantities were already active.

37
00:02:10,583 --> 00:02:15,041
This run confirms existing allocations; it does not apply new quantities.

38
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,751
Next, the test reduces Supplier D's capacity.

39
00:02:20,971 --> 00:02:23,275
The old mix no longer fits that constraint.

40
00:02:23,495 --> 00:02:30,065
Anchor must calculate another allocation, using eligible sources and current limits, or report a shortage.

41
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:40,679
The fresh calculation changes the mix: three hundred and fifty from inventory, four hundred and fifty from D, and two hundred from E.

42
00:02:40,899 --> 00:02:47,171
The plan still covers one thousand units, now costing six thousand five hundred and fifty synthetic dollars.

43
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:53,348
A successful action response is only one piece of evidence.

44
00:02:53,568 --> 00:02:57,579
The simulation persists operational state in the mock E R P.

45
00:02:57,799 --> 00:03:01,745
Anchor reads that state back and compares it with the expected outcome.

46
00:03:01,965 --> 00:03:05,891
Verification must check the stored facts, not just trust the response.

47
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:09,812
Here is the recorded final read-back.

48
00:03:10,032 --> 00:03:14,704
The order is allocated, with twelve scheduled days and zero remaining shortfall.

49
00:03:14,924 --> 00:03:18,892
Inventory was already allocated, so its receipt remains duplicate.

50
00:03:19,112 --> 00:03:23,293
Verification confirms the final active mix in the simulation.

51
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:26,447
Anchor.

52
00:03:26,667 --> 00:03:27,733
Trace the risk.

53
00:03:27,953 --> 00:03:29,020
Rethink the plan.

54
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:30,563
Check the stored outcome.

55
00:03:30,783 --> 00:03:33,663
Built with synthetic data and simulated actions.
